items.statement:
§4.2 setting: K_p = G_1(Z_p), and κ = (k_v,l_v)_{v|p} is a weight satisfying the parity condition k_v − l_v ≡ 0 mod 2 of §3.7.2. Let S_p = {v|p} = I ⊔ I^c be a partition. Write X_1^I := X_1^{≥_I1,≥_{I^c}2}, an open subscheme of X_1, and similarly X_1^{G_1,I} ⊂ X_1^{G_1}.

Theorem 4.2.1. Let T^I = ∏_{w∈I}T_w ∏_{w∈I^c}T_{w,1}. There is a universal constant C, depending only on p and F and not on the tame level K^p, with the following property. Suppose l_w ≥ 2 for all w, k_w − l_w ≥ C for all w ∈ I, and l_w ≥ C for all w ∈ I^c. Then RΓ(X_1^{G_1,I},ω^κ(−D)) carries a locally finite action of T^I, and:
(1) e(T^I)RΓ(X_1^{G_1,I},ω^κ(−D)) is a perfect complex of amplitude [0,#I];
(2) e(T^I)H^0(X_1^{G_1},ω^κ(−D)) → e(T^I)H^0(X_1^{G_1,I},ω^κ(−D)) is an isomorphism;
(3) e(T^I)H^1(X_1^{G_1},ω^κ(−D)) → e(T^I)H^1(X_1^{G_1,I},ω^κ(−D)) is injective;
(4) if furthermore l_w ≥ 3 for all w ∈ I, then e(T^I)RΓ(X_1^{G_1},ω^κ(−D)) → e(T^I)RΓ(X_1^{G_1,I},ω^κ(−D)) is a quasi-isomorphism.
Here e(T^I) is the ordinary projector of §2.11. Parts (2), (3) and (4) also hold for non-cuspidal cohomology.

Remark 4.2.2. The proof shows that the individual Hecke operators act locally finitely under explicit mild bounds on the weights; see Remark 4.2.34.

Proof. Everything except the amplitude is Theorem 4.2.12 with I_a = ∅, I_b = I and J = I^c. The amplitude [0,#I] comes from Proposition 4.2.33.

Lemma 6.5.13. Let w ∈ I^c, let K be a complete valued extension of Q_p with v(p) = 1, and let x = (𝓖, {H_v}_{v∈I}, {H_v ⊂ L_v}_{v∈I^c}, 𝓖 → 𝓖_1) ∈ C_w^{(1)}(Spa(K,O_K)) be a point of the correspondence of §6.5.11 underlying U_{w,1}. Its kernel M_{1,w} ⊂ 𝓖_w[p] is totally isotropic, locally ≅ (O_{F_w}/p)^2, with M_{1,w} ∩ L_w = 0. Write H'_v, L'_v for the images of H_v, L_v in 𝓖_1, and deg for Fargues' degree (§6.5.1). Then:
(1) if v ∈ I, deg H_v = deg H'_v;
(2) if v ∈ I^c and v ≠ w, deg L_v = deg L'_v;
(3) deg L'_w ≥ deg L_w, and in case of equality deg L_w ∈ {0, 1, 2};
(4) deg L'_w = 2 − deg M_{1,w}.

items.note:
Proposition 7.4.7 (the case χ_1 = χ_2 = 1) is a separate item.

Proof of Lemma 7.4.6. Choose σ ∈ I_{F_v} at which the values of χ_1^{±1} and χ_2^{±1} are pairwise distinct. As in Lemma 7.1.3, it suffices that Hom_{E'[G_{F_v}]}(ρ_x, ρ_x(1)) = 0, and any such map preserves the eigenspaces of ρ_x(σ), so it is zero.

Proof of Proposition 7.4.8, as in Taylor's Proposition 3.1:
- Proposition 7.4.17(2) makes (R̃_v^χ)^red flat over O;
- Proposition 7.4.18 makes Spec R̃_v^χ[1/p] connected;
- Lemma 7.4.6 makes its local rings at closed points regular;
- Propositions 7.4.20 and 7.4.21 (separate items) pass between R_v^χ, R̃_v^χ and the space 𝓜(x, y; q_v).
Cited with Proposition 7.4.7 in §7.11 (arXiv p. 226) and §7.12 (arXiv p. 228).

Imprecision. §7.4.19 re-defines 𝓓_v^χ with ν∘ρ = ε^{-1} 'as in §7.4', whereas §7.4.5 has ν∘ρ = ψ|_{G_{F_v}} for any unramified ψ with trivial reduction. The proof of Proposition 7.4.20 works for such ψ after replacing ε by ψ^{-1}, and §7.7 sets ψ = ε^{-1}.

Missing: the nearest planned material is LocalGaloisDeformationRings R08.2 and L7 (fixed inertial type, Steinberg and minimally ramified conditions away from p, with dimensions and components), for representations with fixed determinant. Nothing is planned for GSp_4.

items.statement:
Setting. Assume Hypothesis 7.8.1. Let χ, I ⊆ S_p and 𝔠 be as in §§7.7–7.8, and let (Q, (ᾱ_{v,i})_{v∈Q}) be a Taylor–Wiles datum, possibly empty. The characters of §7.3 — θ_v : I_{F_v} → Λ_v^× for v ∈ I, and θ_{v,1}, θ_{v,2} : I_{F_v} → Λ_v^× for v ∉ I — are extended to G_{F_v} by Art_{F_v}(p) ↦ 1. λ_x denotes the unramified character sending Frob_v to x.
Theorem 7.9.4. There is a unique continuous representation ρ^{χ,I,𝔠,Q} : G_F → GSp_4(T^{χ,I,𝔠,Q}) with the following properties. It is a deformation of ρ̄ of type S^{I,𝔠}_{χ,Q}, and the induced map R_{S^{I,𝔠}_{χ,Q}} → T^{χ,I,𝔠,Q} is a homomorphism of Λ_I[Δ_Q]-algebras. Moreover (matrices are written row by row):
(1) for v ∉ S_p ∪ R ∪ {v_0} ∪ Q, det(X − ρ^{χ,I,𝔠,Q}(Frob_v)) = Q_v(X);
(2) for v ∈ I, ρ^{χ,I,𝔠,Q}|_{G_{F_v}} ≃ [[λ_{α̃_v}θ_v, 0, *, *], [0, λ_{β̃_v}θ_v, *, *], [0, 0, λ_{β̃_v}^{−1}θ_v^{−1}ε^{−1}, 0], [0, 0, 0, λ_{α̃_v}^{−1}θ_v^{−1}ε^{−1}]];
(3) for v ∈ I^c, ρ^{χ,I,𝔠,Q}|_{G_{F_v}} ≃ [[λ_{U_{v,1}}θ_{v,1}, *, *, *], [0, λ_{U_{v,2}/U_{v,1}}θ_{v,2}, *, *], [0, 0, λ_{U_{v,2}/U_{v,1}}^{−1}θ_{v,2}^{−1}ε^{−1}, *], [0, 0, 0, λ_{U_{v,1}}^{−1}θ_{v,1}^{−1}ε^{−1}]].

Hypothesis 7.13.1. ρ : G_F → GSp_4(O) is a continuous representation such that:
(1) F is a totally real field in which the prime p ≥ 3 splits completely (S_p is the set of places above p);
(2) ν∘ρ = ε^{−1};
(3) ρ|_{G_{F_v}} is pure for each finite place v;
(4) for each v ∈ S_p, ρ|_{G_{F_v}} is p-distinguished weight 2 ordinary (Definition 7.3.1), with unit eigenvalues α_v, β_v ∈ E;
(5) there is a finite set R of primes of F not dividing p such that ρ|_{G_{F_v}} is unramified for v ∉ R ∪ S_p, while for v ∈ R: q_v ≡ 1 (mod p), and q_v ≡ 1 (mod 9) if p = 3; ρ̄|_{G_{F_v}} is trivial; and ρ|_{G_{F_v}} has only unipotent ramification;
(6) there is an ordinary cuspidal automorphic representation π = ⊗_vπ_v of GSp_4/F of parallel weight 2 and central character |·|^2 with ρ̄_{π,p} = ρ̄, such that π_v is unramified for v ∉ R ∪ S_p, π_v^{Iw(v)} ≠ 0 for v ∈ R ∪ S_p, and ρ_{π,p}|_{G_{F_v}} is pure for each finite place v;
(7) ρ̄ is vast and tidy.
Remark 7.13.2. Hypothesis 7.13.1 implies Hypothesis 7.8.1 for ρ̄.
(8) By tidiness, as in §7.7, there is an absolutely unramified prime v_0 ∉ S_p ∪ R with q_{v_0} ≢ 1 (mod p) and residue characteristic greater than 5, such that ρ|_{G_{F_{v_0}}} is unramified and no ratio of the (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues of ρ(Frob_{v_0}) is congruent to q_{v_0} modulo λ.
Definition 7.13.4. K^{p,Iw} = ∏_{v∤p,∞}K_v and K^{p,Iw_1} = ∏_{v∤p,∞}K'_v, where K_v = Iw(v) and K'_v = Iw_1(v) for v ∈ R; K_{v_0} = K'_{v_0} = Iw_1(v_0); and K_v = K'_v = GSp_4(O_{F_v}) for v ∉ R ∪ {v_0}.

Let ρ̄ : G_{F,S} → GL_n(k) be continuous and absolutely irreducible, with a fixed determinant lift and local deformation conditions at the places of S. The item covers:
- the framed and strict-equivalence unframed deformation functors on complete Noetherian local coefficient rings with residue field k;
- their representability (unframed under absolute irreducibility) and universal representations;
- the comparison of the T-framed and unframed rings by a formally smooth power-series map in the correct number of variables;
- Kisin's presentation (Moduli of finite flat group schemes, and modularity, Lemma 3.2.2): for T ⊇ S_p, with unrestricted lifts at S ∖ T, a surjection onto the T-framed global ring from a power series ring in g = h^1_{𝓢,T}(ad^0ρ̄) − h^0(F_S/F, adρ̄) + Σ_{v∈T} h^0(F_v, adρ̄) variables over the completed tensor product of the local lifting rings at T.
The paper uses these only in their GSp_4 form with fixed similitude character (Definitions 7.1.2–7.1.5, Lemma 7.1.6, Proposition 7.2.1), saying that the proofs are identical.

Setting of §4.2.11. K_p = G_1(Z_p); I_a, I_b, J ⊆ S_p are pairwise disjoint; X_1^{I_{a,b},J} = X_1^{≤_{I_a}1,≥_{I_b}1,≥_J2}. Take w ∈ I_b, I = I' ⊔ {w}, I'_a = I_a and I'_b = I' ∩ I_b. Let K' = K^pK'_p with K'_p = ∏_{v≠w}K_v × Kli(w), and K'' = K^pK''_p with K''_p = ∏_{v≠w}K_v × Par(w). For k_w ≥ l_w ≥ 2 there are Klingen type cohomological correspondences T'_w: p_2^*(ω^κ|_{X_{K''}}) → p_1^!(ω^κ|_{X_K}) and T''_w: p_1^*(ω^κ|_{X_K}) → p_2^!(ω^κ|_{X_{K''}}), with T_w = T'_w∘T''_w (§3.9.20). They reduce modulo p by Lemma 3.8.10, and restrict to the preimage X^{I_{a,b},J}_{K',1} of X_1^{I_{a,b},J} whenever w ∉ I_a.

Ordinary locus at w. (X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K',1})^{=_w2} = (…)^{=_w2,et} ⊔ (…)^{=_w2,net}. On the first, the kernel of the universal isogeny p_1^*𝓖 → p_2^*𝓖, of degree p^3, has multiplicative rank 1; on the second it has multiplicative rank 2. The isogeny p_2^*𝓖 → p_1^*𝓖, of degree p, has multiplicative kernel over the first and is étale over the second. This gives T'_w = T'_{w,et} + T'_{w,net}, projecting on the et, resp. net, components, and T''_w = T''_{w,et} + T''_{w,net}, projecting on the net, resp. et, components.

Lemma 4.2.14. If l_w ≥ 2 and k_w ≥ 3, then over (X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K',1})^{=_w2}:
- T'_{w,net} = T''_{w,net} = 0; by [Pil20, Lem. 7.1.1, 7.1.2], T'_{w,net} is divisible by p^{k_w−2} and T''_{w,net} by p^{l_w−1};
- p_1^*Ha(𝓖_w)∘T'_{w,et} = T'_{w,et}∘p_2^*Ha(𝓖_w) as maps p_2^*ω^κ → p_1^!(ω^κ ⊗ det ω^{p−1}_{𝓖_w});
- p_2^*Ha(𝓖_w)∘T''_{w,et} = T''_{w,et}∘p_1^*Ha(𝓖_w) as maps p_1^*ω^κ → p_2^!(ω^κ ⊗ det ω^{p−1}_{𝓖_w}).

Lemma 4.2.15. For l_w ≥ 2 and k_w ≥ 3, the same two identities hold for T'_w and T''_w as maps of locally free sheaves on X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K',1}. By Cohen–Macaulayness they reduce to Lemma 4.2.14.

Rank one locus at w. (X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K',1})^{=_w1} is the union of open and closed pieces =_w1,m, =_w1,et and =_w1,00, on which the kernel of p_2^*𝓖 → p_1^*𝓖 is étale, multiplicative, resp. bi-connected. Also (X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K'',1})^{=_w1} = (…)^{=_w1,00} ⊔ (…)^{=_w1,m-et}, according as the kernel of the quasi-polarization 𝓖_w → 𝓖_w^D does not, resp. does, contain a multiplicative group [Pil20, Lem. 7.4.2.3]. Then p_2((…)_{K',1}^{=_w1,00}) ⊆ (…)_{K'',1}^{=_w1,00} (4.2.17), and, on topological spaces, p_2((…)^{=_w1,m} ∪ (…)^{=_w1,et}) ⊆ (…)_{K'',1}^{=_w1,m-et} (4.2.18). This gives T'_w = T'_{w,m} + T'_{w,et} + T'_{w,00} and T''_w = T''_{w,m} + T''_{w,et} + T''_{w,00}.

Lemma 4.2.19 [Pil20, Prop. 7.4.2.1]. For l_w ≥ p+1 and k_w ≥ 2p+3:
- p_1^*Ha'(𝓖_w)∘T'_{w,et} = T'_{w,et}∘p_2^*Ha'(𝓖_w), as maps p_2^*ω^κ|_{(X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K'',1})^{=_w1}} → p_1^!(ω^κ ⊗ det ω^{p^2−1}_{𝓖_w})|_{(X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K,1})^{=_w1}};
- p_2^*Ha'(𝓖_w)∘T''_{w,et} = T''_{w,et}∘p_1^*Ha'(𝓖_w), as maps p_1^*ω^κ|_{(X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K,1})^{=_w1}} → p_2^!(ω^κ ⊗ det ω^{p^2−1}_{𝓖_w})|_{(X^{I'_{a,b},J}_{K'',1})^{=_w1}};
- T'_{w,m} = T'_{w,00} = 0 and T''_{w,m} = 0;
- if l_w ≥ p+2, then T''_{w,00} = 0.

Lemma 6.5.12. Let w ∈ I and let x = (𝓖, {H_v}_{v∈I}, {H_v ⊂ L_v}_{v∈I^c}, 𝓖 → 𝓖_1) ∈ C_w^{(1)}(Spa(K,O_K)), with kernel M_{1,w} ⊂ 𝓖_w[p^2] (§6.5.11), images H'_v and L'_v in 𝓖_1, and deg Fargues' degree. Then:
(1) if v ∈ I and v ≠ w, deg H_v = deg H'_v;
(2) if v ∈ I^c, deg L_v = deg L'_v;
(3) deg H'_w ≥ deg H_w, and in case of equality deg H_w ∈ {0,1};
(4) deg H'_w = 1 − deg M_{1,w}/M_{1,w}[p];
(5) deg M_{1,w}[p]/pM_{1,w} = 1, and deg pM_{1,w} ≥ deg M_{1,w}/M_{1,w}[p];
(6) for ε ≥ 0, if deg M_{1,w} ≤ 3 − 2ε then deg H'_w ≥ ε.

Lemma 6.5.18. Let w ∈ I^c and let J_v (v ≠ w) be intervals as in §6.5.16. For all 1 > ε' ≥ ε > 0 the restriction map
  H^i(𝓧_{K^pK_p(I)}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [2−ε', 2]), ω^κ)^{fs−U_{w,1}} → H^i(𝓧_{K^pK_p(I)}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [2−ε, 2]), ω^κ)^{fs−U_{w,1}}
is an isomorphism.

Lemma 6.5.20. Let w ∈ I and let J_v (v ≠ w) be intervals. For all 1 > ε ≥ ε' > 0 the restriction map
  H^i(𝓧_{K^pK_p(I)}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [1−ε, 1]), ω^κ)^{fs−U_{w,2}} → H^i(𝓧_{K^pK_p(I)}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [1−ε', 1]), ω^κ)^{fs−U_{w,2}}
is an isomorphism.

Proposition 6.5.22. Let w ∈ I^c, let J_v (v ≠ w) be intervals, let n ≥ 1 and 0 < ε < ε' (with ε ≤ 1 as in §6.5.21), and put N = n(l_w(1−ε') − 3). Sheaves on the opens below are regarded as sheaves on 𝓧_{K_p(I)K^p}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,2]) by pushforward. There is a morphism of such sheaves
  Ũ : (t_{w,n,1})_*(t_{w,n,2})^*((ω^κ)^{++}|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [1+ε,2])}) → (ω^κ/p^N(ω^κ)^{++})|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,2])}
with two properties:
- composed with restriction to 𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [1+ε,2]), it is the Hecke correspondence U^n_{w,1} on that open reduced modulo p^N(ω^κ)^{++};
- precomposed with the restriction from (t_{w,n,1})_*(t_{w,n,2})^*((ω^κ)^{++}|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,2])}), it is U^n_{w,1} on 𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,2]) reduced modulo p^N(ω^κ)^{++}.
The paper displays this as a commutative diagram.

items.note:
Proof in the paper, locally at x:
- split t_{w,n,1}^{−1}(U) = V ⊔ W according to whether |δ_{M_{w,n}}| < or > |p^{n(1−ε'')}|;
- on V the correspondence is divisible by p^{n(l_w(1−ε'')−3)} (Pilloni 2020, Lem. 14.6.1);
- t_{w,n,2}(W) lies in the [1+ε,2] neighbourhood, by Lemma 6.5.13(4) and the monotonicity of the degrees of the graded pieces M_{w,i+1}/M_{w,i}.
The proof prints l_v for l_w three times: twice with ε'' on arXiv p. 160 and once with ε' on p. 161.
Status missing: PAPER-PILLONI-20 records its Klingen-level model (Prop. 14.6.1) as missing.

Proof in the paper:
- choose ε' with l_w(1−ε') − 3 > v(a) for all roots a, and use Lemma 6.5.18 to assume 0 < ε < ε';
- rescale f to be integral;
- Q(U_{w,1})^n = Q(Ũ_{w,1})^n factors through the [0,2] neighbourhood modulo p^{nα} by Proposition 6.5.22, where α = inf_a {l_w(1−ε') − 3 − v(a)};
- ext(f) is the projective system f_n.
PAPER-PILLONI-20 records its Klingen-level analogue (Cor. 14.6.2) as missing.

items.statement:
Proposition 6.5.24. Let w ∈ I, let J_v (v ≠ w) be intervals, let n ≥ 1 and 0 < ε < ε', and put N = n(l_w + k_w − 3 − 2ε'k_w). Sheaves on the opens below are regarded as sheaves on 𝓧_{K_p(I)K^p}(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,1]) by pushforward. There is a morphism of such sheaves
  (t_{w,n,1})_*(t_{w,n,2})^*((ω^κ)^{++}|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [ε,1])}) → (ω^κ/p^N(ω^κ)^{++})|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,1])}
with two properties:
- composed with restriction to 𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [ε,1]), it is U^n_{w,2} there reduced modulo p^N(ω^κ)^{++};
- precomposed with the restriction from (t_{w,n,1})_*(t_{w,n,2})^*((ω^κ)^{++}|_{𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,1])}), it is U^n_{w,2} on 𝓧(∏_{v≠w}J_v × [0,1]) reduced modulo p^N(ω^κ)^{++}.

Lemma 6.5.28. Choose monic polynomials P_v (v|p) such that:
- if v ∈ I^c, every root a of P_v satisfies v(a) < l_v − 3;
- if v ∈ I, every root a of P_v satisfies v(a) < k_v + l_v − 3.
Put U_v = U_{v,1} for v ∈ I^c and U_v = U_{v,2} for v ∈ I. If S ⊂ T ⊂ S_p, then the restriction map
  H^i(𝓧^{G_1}_{K_p(I)K^p}(J(T,ε)), ω^κ)[P_v(U_v) = 0]_{v∈S_p} → H^i(𝓧^{G_1}_{K_p(I)K^p}(J(S,ε)), ω^κ)[P_v(U_v) = 0]_{v∈S_p}
is an isomorphism.

(Proof of Proposition 6.6.1, #I = 1.) Let π : 𝓧_{K_p(I)K^p} → 𝓧^*_{K_p(I)K^p} be the projection to the minimal compactification; then R^iπ_*ω^κ(−D) = 0 for i > 0. The image 𝓧^{*,mult}_{K_p(I)K^p} of 𝓧^{mult}_{K_p(I)K^p} admits an affinoid cover U_1 ∪ U_2. Hence:
- RΓ(𝓧^{mult}_{K_p(I)K^p}, ω^κ(−D)) is represented by the Čech complex H^0(U_1, ω^κ(−D)) ⊕ H^0(U_2, ω^κ(−D)) → H^0(U_1∩U_2, ω^κ(−D)) of Banach spaces, with norms from a formal model.
- RΓ(𝓧^{mult,†}_{K_p(I)K^p}, ω^κ(−D)) is represented by its subcomplex of overconvergent sections, where ω^{κ,†}(U) := colim_V ω^κ(V) over the strict neighbourhoods V of U.
- The quotient topology on H^1(𝓧^{mult}, ω^κ(−D)) coincides with the topology for which H^1(𝔛^I_{K_p(I)K^p}, ω^κ(−D)) is open and bounded.
Consequences:
- H^1(𝓧^{mult,†}, ω^κ(−D)) → H^1(𝓧^{mult}, ω^κ(−D)) has dense image;
- U^I is continuous on H^1(𝓧^{mult}, ω^κ(−D));
- the projection H^1(𝓧^{G_1,mult}, ω^κ(−D)) → e(U^I)H^1(𝓧^{G_1,mult}, ω^κ(−D)) is continuous;
- e(U^I)H^1(𝓧^{G_1,mult,†}, ω^κ(−D)) → e(U^I)H^1(𝓧^{G_1,mult}, ω^κ(−D)) has dense image.

items.note:
For F = Q, Pilloni 2020 Prop. 14.9.1 proves the inequality for H^0 and H^1 classes. The paper says the argument 'generalizes without difficulty' but does not spell it out, so the authors acknowledge the omission. They name substitutes that are in force in the rest of the paper:
- localizing at p-adically integral eigenvalues of T_{v,1} and U_{Kli(v),1};
- for H^1, deducing the inequality from H^0 after a non-Eisenstein localization, where the Euler characteristic vanishes;
- for H^0, using eigenvarieties.
Status missing: no layer plans a Mazur-type inequality for Hecke parameters in coherent cohomology, and PAPER-PILLONI-20 records its Prop. 14.9.1 as missing.

The print omits the congruence k_v ≡ l_v ≡ 2 (mod p−1), but it is required. §4.4.1 defines the specialization f_κ : Λ_I → Z_p only for such κ, and Theorems 4.6.1(3) and 6.3.16 are stated only for them (sourceIssues E90, a misprint that affects nothing, since every application satisfies the congruence). The paper leaves EC undefined; the inequalities in the proof fix the sign as h^0 − h^1.
Proof in the paper:
- EC(†) ≤ EC(mult,†) by Corollary 6.4.3 (with Proposition 6.3.7), with equality if k_v − l_v ≥ 3 as printed, though the argument gives equality only when k_v − l_v ≥ 3N with N = #I + 2#I^c (sourceIssues E83, see item 263), which the reduction to large weights below allows;
- EC(mult,†) ≤ EC(M) by Proposition 6.6.1;
- EC(†) and EC(M) are locally constant in κ (Theorems 6.3.16 and 4.6.1), which reduces the claim to large weights;
- there, Theorem 6.5.8, the T/U comparison claim and Theorem 4.6.1(4) give EC(mult,†) ≥ EC(M), hence EC(†) ≥ EC(M).
As stated (unlocalized), the proof depends on applying Theorem 3.10.1(2) to unlocalized cohomology, as item 287 explains for the claim. HigherHidaAndColemanTheory (Hida and Coleman families, period modules, and family L-functions, Part II: higher Hida and higher Coleman theory for coherent cohomology), to which this item is routed, plans the proposition after localizing both sides at a non-Eisenstein maximal ideal. Status missing: PAPER-PILLONI-20 records the F = Q analogue (Lemmas 14.8.3–14.8.4) as missing.

